Act as a senior product manager and product designer. Inspect the available conversation history and source code, but scope the spec to the product surface, flow, or feature the user changed or created. Do not summarize or document the entire repository simply because it is available.

The repository may use any framework. Inspect its package metadata, build configuration, application entry points, router, and existing source conventions before choosing how to expose live previews. Do not assume React, TanStack Router, or a particular directory structure.

Create or update the framework-neutral structured document at ${s}. Before writing, check whether ${s} already exists. If it does not, also check whether the legacy ${n} exists and use it as prior product context:
- If either file exists, read it first and treat it as prior product context and the authored starting point for this revision.
- Preserve its supported decisions, structure, wording, and manual edits unless the current request or prototype clearly requires a change. When the evidence is ambiguous, preserve the existing content.
- Revise the document incrementally. Update or add only the sections affected by the current request or relevant prototype changes; do not discard or rewrite unaffected content.
- Ignore the existing spec and start over only when the user explicitly asks to start over, regenerate from scratch, or ignore the previous spec.
- If neither file exists, create the initial spec from the current prototype and conversation.

Write ${s} as valid JSON with this shape:
{
  "version": 1,
  "title": "Product spec title",
  "blocks": [
    { "id": "intent", "type": "heading", "level": 2, "text": "Product intent" },
    { "id": "intent-body", "type": "paragraph", "text": "..." },
    { "id": "requirements", "type": "list", "style": "bullet", "items": [{ "id": "requirement-1", "text": "..." }] },
    { "id": "states", "type": "table", "columns": [{ "id": "state", "label": "State" }, { "id": "behavior", "label": "Behavior" }], "rows": [{ "id": "default", "cells": { "state": "Default", "behavior": "..." } }] },
    { "id": "decision", "type": "callout", "tone": "decision", "title": "Decision", "text": "..." },
    { "id": "preview", "type": "preview", "title": "Implemented experience", "route": "/actual-app-route", "description": "Uses the running implementation." }
  ]
}

Supported block types are heading, paragraph, list, table, callout, and preview. Heading levels are 1 through 6. List style is bullet or numbered. Callout tone is info, decision, warning, or question. Every block, list item, table column, and table row must have a stable unique id. All authored prose belongs in these structured fields so it remains directly editable and persistable.

The document is a working product spec:
- Treat this as a design specification, not a prototype demo or a narrative document. Its primary job is to break the changed product surface into inspectable implementation units.
- Begin by inventorying the net-new or materially changed components, layouts, states, and flows in the relevant product surface. Do not inventory unchanged infrastructure or the rest of the repository.
- Create a component section for every net-new or materially changed UI component. Each section must name the component, explain its responsibility and interaction behavior, document its meaningful states and variants, and include live preview blocks for the real implementation whenever the project can run it.
- Use separate preview blocks for meaningful component states such as default, hover, selected, expanded, loading, empty, error, and responsive variants when those states exist. Do not collapse distinct states into one whole-product screenshot.
- After the component-level sections, document how those components compose into layouts and user flows. A full-flow preview may be included as a secondary overview, but it must not replace the component-by-component specification.
- Explain the product intent, user flow, states, requirements, edge cases, open questions, and non-goals where the available evidence supports them.
- Clearly label inferences and unknowns.
- Do not mention Figma unless the product being prototyped is itself related to Figma.
- Do not use the word "collaborative" as a product-quality requirement.

For each preview block:
- For a component section, render the actual component in isolation at a useful inspection size. Do not use the existing whole-application route merely because it is already available.
- Prefer an existing route only when it already isolates the exact component or state being specified.
- Otherwise, create the smallest framework-native preview harness using the project's existing framework, dependencies, providers, styles, and router conventions. Seed each harness with the props, fixtures, and local state needed to make that exact component state visible. It is acceptable to modify application code only as needed to register that dedicated harness.
- Import and render the actual implementation. Never recreate an implemented component, layout, or flow as HTML/CSS merely for the spec.
- Never substitute a scaled replica of the whole prototype for component-level previews. Reuse the real implementation 1:1 and isolate it through routing or a preview harness.
- Do not add a framework or dependency just for the spec.
- Use a same-origin route beginning with /. Hash routes such as /#/spec are valid. Do not use an absolute or protocol-relative URL.
- Keep generated harness code clearly isolated and avoid changing production behavior.
- If no live route can be made safely, omit the preview block and explain the limitation in a question or warning callout instead of fabricating a static recreation.

Use this request as additional focus:
${t}

